namespace Linx {

/**

\addtogroup arrays

\section arrays-intro Introduction

Linx provides classes and services for handling one-dimensional (1D) and multi-dimensional (ND) arrays.
They contain an arbitrary number of elements of the same type, laid out in memory according to infrastructure-dependent policies.
1D arrays are implemented by the `Sequence` class template, while ND arrays are implemented by the `Image` class template.
The default data container type is generally a `Kokkos::View`, but other containers can be used if needed.
Elements can be accessed and modified using indexing operators.
Both classes support operations that modify the array in place and operations that create a new instance of the array.
Such operations encompass element-wise arithmetic or mathematical functions, reductions,
as well as global transforms such as filtering and Fourier transforms.
Slicing and domain restriction enable operations over arbitrary regions.

```cpp
template <typename T, int N, typename TContainer = ImageContainer<T, N>>
class Image;

template <typename T, typename TContainer = SequenceContainer<T>>
class Sequence;
```

\section arrays-memory Memory management

The semantics of `Sequence` and `Image` are similar to those of `std::shared_ptr`:
memory is managed automatically, and can be shared between multiple objects without copying the underlying data.
Copy constructor and copy assignment operator perform shallow copy.
Memory is released when the last reference to the data is destroyed.

The memory layout (alignment, padding and element storage order) depends on the underlying machine architecture and the underlying data container type.
By default, `Kokkos::View` uses a machine-dependent layout, but this can be changed by specifying a different layout policy.
For example, `Raster` uses a contiguous, row-major ordered layout, which is compatible with most legacy libraries.

The container can be accessed through the `container()` method in order to interface with other Kokkos-based libraries.
It is also possible to obtain a pointer to the raw data using the `data()` method, which is useful for interoperability with other libraries.

\section arrays-datatypes Data types

Both `Sequence` and `Image` support numerical data types, including integers, floating-point numbers, and complex numbers, as well as strings.
Custom data types can also be used, with constraints inherited from the underlying data container type, such as `Kokkos::View`.
Typically, it is not possible by default to use non-POD types.

\section arrays-sequence-size Sequence size

There are two kinds of sizes for sequences:

- Static: the size is known at compile time and is specified as template parameter `N`;
- Dynamic: the size is not known at compile time and is specified at construction, with template parameter `N` set to `-1`.

Static-size sequences ressemble standard arrays and are more efficient than dynamic-size sequences,
while dynamic-size sequences ressemble non-resizable standard vectors.
The rank of a sequence, i.e. the number of indices needed to identify an element, is always 1.

\snippet{trimleft} arrays_example.cpp sequence_size

Empty sequences are supported, too, either with static or dynamic (null) size.

\snippet{trimleft} arrays_example.cpp sequence_null_size

\section arrays-image-rank Image rank

The term rank refers to the number of axes in an image.
The rank can be set statically or dynamically (with `N = -1`), similar to a sequence size,
but the number of elements is generally specified for each axis in the constructor.

\snippet{trimleft} arrays_example.cpp image_rank

The maximum rank of an image is determined by the underlying data container type, which is 8 for `Kokkos::View` and 7 for `Kokkos::DynRankView`.
However, many services offered by Kokkos are limited to views with up to 6 dimensions.
The Linx library itself supports arbitrary ranks.

0-dimensional images are supported.
As opposed to tensor libraries, they are not treated as scalars, but as empty containers.

\snippet{trimleft} arrays_example.cpp image_null_rank

\section arrays-creation Array creation

Image of up to 3 dimensions can be created from initializer lists representing rows using function `rowwise()`.

\snippet{trimleft} arrays_example.cpp rowwise

\section arrays-access Element access

Both `Sequence` and `Image` classes provide access to their elements through indexing operators.
For example, `sequence[i]` accesses the i-th element of a `Sequence`,
and `image(i, j)` accesses the element at position (i, j) in an `Image`.

`Sequence`s are always iterable, while `Image`s are iterable only if the underlying data is contiguous in memory.
Typically, this is the case for `Raster`.

\snippet{trimleft} arrays_example.cpp access

*/

}
